========================================================= -->

<section class="section">

  <div class="section-heading">

    <h2>11. Questions to answer before committing</h2>

  </div>


  <details open>

    <summary>
      Can I actually finish this course?
    </summary>

    <div class="details-content">

      Compare the required weekly hours against your real schedule.
      A theoretically excellent program that cannot fit your life is
      not necessarily the best choice.

    </div>

  </details>


  <details>

    <summary>
      Does this course teach the skills my target jobs require?
    </summary>

    <div class="details-content">

      Copy 10–20 relevant job descriptions into a spreadsheet.
      Count recurring technologies and responsibilities.
      Use that frequency to identify your highest-value learning gaps.

    </div>

  </details>


  <details>

    <summary>
      What will I have to learn outside the course?
    </summary>

    <div class="details-content">

      Look for missing areas such as Git, deployment, accessibility,
      testing, APIs, databases, WordPress, React, SQL or cloud tools.
      Missing skills should become part of the plan rather than surprises
      after graduation.

    </div>

  </details>


  <details>

    <summary>
      What can I build from each section?
    </summary>

    <div class="details-content">

      Every major topic should trigger a small application exercise.
      Every several related topics should produce a portfolio project.
      This prevents passive course consumption.

    </div>

  </details>


  <details>

    <summary>
      When should I start applying?
    </summary>

    <div class="details-content">

      Do not automatically wait until the entire curriculum is complete.
      Once you have several credible projects and can satisfy a meaningful
      portion of a job description, begin applying while continuing to learn.
      Early applications also reveal what employers are actually asking for.

    </div>

  </details>

</section>


